                <pb facs="d145-01" n="1" xml:id="d145-01"/>

                <head>145. Memorandum From the Executive Secretary (Eliot) to the President’s Assistant for National
                    Security Affairs (Kissinger)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d145-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d145-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1970-08-24"
                        >August 24, 1970</date>
                </dateline>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>“Straws in the Wind”—Changes in Tunisian Foreign Policy</item>
                </list>
                <p>Enclosed for your information is a memorandum prepared for the Acting Secretary
                    by the Bureau of African Affairs.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>Theodore Eliot, Jr.</signed>
                </closer>



                <xi:include href="d145-a1.xml" xpointer="d145-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d145-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d145-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files
                    1970–73, POL 1 TUN. Confidential. Cleared by
                        Wisner. In Airgram A–155 from Tunis,
                    August 18, the Embassy noted that the “special relationship” that Washington had
                    enjoyed with Tunis was likely to end with the demise or retirement of Bourguiba. (Ibid., POL 15–1 TUN)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d145-f2" n="2" xml:id="d145-f2-content">
                <p>Eliot sent an information memorandum prepared in the Bureau of African Affairs,
                    which observed that, under Foreign Minister Masmoudi, Tunisia had reasserted its non-aligned standing and
                    harmonized its foreign policy with that of the Arab and Third World
                    countries.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
